  "id": "Compound::DB06203 - Gene::1576",
  "verbalization": "Alogliptin, a dipeptidyl peptidase 4 (DPP4) inhibitor, is linked to Cyp3a4 through multiple pathways. The evidence suggests that Alogliptin, as part of the DPP4 inhibitor class, binds to Cyp3a4 via indirect routes. Both Alogliptin and Sitagliptin, another DPP4 inhibitor, are known to interact with Cyp3a4, indicating a shared mechanism of action. This is further supported by the presence of a common ancestor in the DPP4 inhibitor class, which connects Alogliptin to Cyp3a4 through the heterocyclic compound category. The observed binding of Cyp3a4 to Alogliptin and other DPP4 inhibitors implies a potential role in drug metabolism, as Cyp3a4 is a key enzyme in drug metabolism. The evidence also highlights a possible mechanism involving the interaction of Alogliptin with Cyp3a4 through the DPP4 inhibitor class, which may influence drug clearance and efficacy. The integration of these pathways supports a mechanistic link between Alogliptin and Cyp3a4, though the exact nature of the interaction remains to be fully elucidated.",
